From b0c90501714e71b0a303045c9492d45df6a3f40b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NayiemW <37852059+NayiemW@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 04:54:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Validate the genesis block at start

DatabaseInteraction.initialise() now checks the configured genesis
block before it stores or uses it. The block must pass Block.verify(),
so patch 43 (payloadLength) and patch 44 (BIP340 block signature) apply
to it, and every genesis transaction's handler must be active at height
1 (the registry's getActivatedHandlerForData, run at height 1), so
patches 01 and 02 apply to it too. Otherwise the node logs "Invalid
genesis block: <errors>", terminates with "Invalid genesis block" and
exits with code 1, as patch 45 does for an inconsistent state. This
decides which genesis block a node accepts.

A genesis that BlockFactory.fromJson cannot decode is refused the same
way, with the error "the genesis block could not be decoded: <reason>".
One example is a genesis transaction whose JSON id does not match its
bytes: Solar grants the genesis exemptions (a fee of 0) by the JSON
ids, so the strict decode of the bytes refuses the fee and throws.
Without this, the throw reached the outer catch of initialise(), which
calls terminate() without an exit and leaves the process running. The
exit is in a finally around the terminate, so a terminate that throws
(a service provider whose dispose fails) still exits.

In s1-ref-v1 the genesis was built with BlockFactory.fromJson and never
checked: a genesis with a 1/5 transaction, a wrong payloadLength or a
legacy-Schnorr block signature was stored and applied.

One check of Block.verify() is left out for the genesis: "Invalid block
timestamp" (timestamp > now + blockTime). It reads the wall clock, and
the genesis timestamp 0 is the epoch, so it fails whenever a node
starts more than one block time before its network's epoch. A devnet
that starts its node 90 s before the epoch does exactly that: a fresh
genesis read before its epoch verifies with that one error only. With
the check kept, every new devnet would refuse to start.

Checked on a generated IceRoot devnet (nethash c9b03ab9...bcca,
genesis id fbdd5afb...605e, 107 transactions): its genesis passes this
check about 80 s before its epoch, and a relay started on it 887 s
before its epoch comes up (the generator key shows balance 0). Solar's
own mainnet and testnet preset genesis blocks fail (patch 43 already
noted their payloadLength); those networks are offline for good.

Checked on a running node of the same devnet: with payloadLength + 1
in genesisBlock.json it logs "Invalid genesis block: Failed to verify
block signature; Invalid payload length" and the relay process exits
at once.

Test: patches/tests/46-genesis-validated.test.js (stub collaborators,
the real handler classes in a registry, process.exit stubbed). A valid
genesis is accepted, also 90 s before its epoch. A 1/5 genesis (whose
block verifies), a genesis with payloadLength 33 for 1 transaction, and
a legacy-Schnorr-signed genesis each terminate with "Invalid genesis
block" and exit 1 (base: accepted). With a terminate() that throws, the
process still exits 1. A genesis whose issuance transaction has a
tampered JSON id makes fromJson throw, and initialise() terminates with
"Invalid genesis block" and exits 1 (before this patch: terminates with
"Failed to initialise database service" and does not exit). The real
genesis under $DEVNET_CRYPTO is accepted, and so is a genesis under
network byte 30: the test resets Solar's isGenesisTransaction cache
under a byte that differs from the target's, because the testnet
preset has byte 30 itself.

Patch: 46
Consensus: yes
